# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FlowCMS — example environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development (`bun run dev` / `next dev`), or to
# `.env` for Docker Compose. NEVER commit the copy — only this example file is
# tracked. Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER; none of them are real secrets.
#
# Generate each secret with 32 random bytes, base64:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Variables are grouped by LIFETIME, which is the thing that decides where a
# value is allowed to live:
#
#   1. Bootstrap — env only. These decide how the process boots and what it
#      trusts, so they cannot be stored in the database the process is booting.
#   2. Operational — env provides the bootstrap value; Admin > Settings can
#      override it per field at runtime.
#   3. Database only — configured entirely in the admin panel, never here
#      (Google Search Console OAuth, Bing Webmaster key, PageSpeed key, brand,
#      business profile, SEO templates).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === 1. Bootstrap (env only) ===============================================

# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# FlowCMS supports four databases. Pick one with DATABASE_DIALECT and point
# DATABASE_URL at it. The dialect is explicit rather than guessed: MySQL and
# MariaDB share the mysql:// scheme while being separately tested products, so
# inferring one would make your supported database a runtime coin-flip.
#
#   DATABASE_DIALECT=sqlite      DATABASE_URL=file:/data/app.db
#   DATABASE_DIALECT=postgresql  DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@postgres:5432/flowcms
#   DATABASE_DIALECT=mysql       DATABASE_URL=mysql://user:pass@mysql:3306/flowcms
#   DATABASE_DIALECT=mariadb     DATABASE_URL=mysql://user:pass@mariadb:3306/flowcms
#
# A contradictory pair (postgresql + file:) is refused at startup rather than
# resolved, and the password is redacted from every error and log line.
#
# The same image runs all four; migrations for the selected dialect are applied
# automatically at container start.
#
# DATABASE_PATH is gone. Its replacement is DATABASE_URL=file:...
DATABASE_DIALECT=sqlite
DATABASE_URL=file:data/app.db

# Connection pool size for PostgreSQL/MySQL/MariaDB. SQLite ignores it.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# --- Auth.js ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Signs and encrypts the session JWT.
#
# REQUIRED, and the highest-value secret in FlowCMS. Whoever holds it can mint a
# session for any account — owner included — without a password, a CAPTCHA or
# the rate limiter. It is not a hardening detail; it IS the authentication.
#
# THE PLACEHOLDER BELOW IS REFUSED, on purpose. It is published in this
# repository, so a deployment using it has a session-signing key that anyone
# reading the source already knows. FlowCMS validates the value, and a missing
# or weak one means: not-ready on /api/ready (auth: "missing" | "unsafe"), a
# redacted diagnostic at startup, first-run setup refuses to complete, and
# Auth.js is given no secret at all rather than the rejected one — so no session
# can be signed or verified with it.
#
# At least 24 characters with at least 8 distinct characters, generated randomly:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# ROTATING IT SIGNS EVERY USER OUT. That is expected, and it is far safer than
# continuing with a compromised signing key. There is deliberately no keyring
# and no previous-secret support: a rotation that kept old sessions valid would
# keep the compromised key valid too.
#
# EVERY REPLICA MUST GET THE SAME VALUE. FlowCMS never generates one at runtime,
# precisely so that two instances cannot end up with different keys — a session
# minted by one would be rejected by the next, and every restart would sign
# everyone out.
AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-with-32-random-bytes-base64

# --- Login CAPTCHA ---------------------------------------------------------
# HMAC key for the signed login CAPTCHA challenge.
#
# REQUIRED for a functional installation. There is no "CAPTCHA disabled" state
# in FlowCMS: without this key no challenge can be issued or verified, so nobody
# can sign in to the admin panel at all. A missing value does not relax the
# login — it closes it.
#
# The key is what makes the challenge cookie trustworthy: only the server can
# produce a valid signature. A short or guessable value lets an attacker mint
# their own challenge, which removes the CAPTCHA rather than weakening it. So it
# must be at least 24 characters with at least 8 distinct characters, and the
# placeholder below is REFUSED on purpose — copying this file is not configuring
# it.
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# An installation without a usable value reports itself not-ready on
# /api/ready (captcha: "missing" | "unsafe"), logs a redacted diagnostic at
# startup, answers /api/captcha with 503 instead of an opaque 500, and refuses
# to complete first-run setup — because setup that succeeds into an admin panel
# nobody can sign into is a broken installation, and setup closes permanently.
#
# Rotating it invalidates every outstanding CAPTCHA challenge, which is at most
# five minutes of inconvenience.
CAPTCHA_SECRET=replace-me-with-32-random-bytes-base64

# --- Shareable draft previews ----------------------------------------------
# HMAC key for `/blog/<slug>?preview=<token>` links. OPTIONAL and fails closed:
# with it unset, token creation returns null and verification rejects
# everything, so the feature is simply off. Rotating it invalidates every
# outstanding preview link at once — the only revocation mechanism there is.
PREVIEW_SECRET=

# --- First-run setup -------------------------------------------------------
# Authorizes the one-time web setup form at /setup on a fresh installation.
#
# A newly deployed FlowCMS has no accounts, so for a few minutes it is a machine
# on the internet that will hand ownership to whoever asks first. This token is
# what makes "whoever asks first" mean "whoever deployed it".
#
# GENERATE ONE. Do not invent one, and do not copy this file's value — there
# isn't one, deliberately:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Rules, enforced at the endpoint: at least 24 characters and at least 8
# distinct characters. There is no complexity requirement, because a random
# 32-byte token is stronger than anything a complexity rule would force.
#
# OPTIONAL, and it fails CLOSED. Left unset, web setup is locked and says so;
# the application still boots and serves normally, and an operator with server
# access creates the first owner directly:
#   FLOWCMS_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com FLOWCMS_OWNER_PASSWORD='…' \
#     node scripts/bootstrap-owner.mjs
#
# It is only consulted while the installation is uninitialized. Once setup
# completes — by either route — /setup and /api/setup return 404 and this
# variable does nothing. Removing it afterwards is safe and tidy.
#
# It is never written to the database, never logged, and never accepted from a
# URL — only from the setup form's POST body.
FLOWCMS_SETUP_TOKEN=

# --- Redis -----------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. Backs the admin list/detail cache and login rate limiting. Every
# call fails soft, so an unset or unreachable Redis degrades to hitting the
# database directly rather than erroring.
#
# SECURITY NOTE: with Redis unavailable, login rate limiting falls back to a
# per-process in-memory limiter. That still throttles a single instance but
# does NOT coordinate across replicas. Configure Redis for any multi-replica
# deployment.
REDIS_URL=


# --- Content Security Policy -----------------------------------------------
# One of: report-only (default), enforce, off.
#
# The full CSP ships REPORT-ONLY because Next.js inlines its hydration scripts
# and this app has no per-request nonce mechanism yet, so the policy has to
# permit inline script. Report-only lets you see what enforcement would break
# before switching it on. Clickjacking protection (frame-ancestors / 
# X-Frame-Options) is enforced in every mode, including "off".
FLOWCMS_CSP=report-only

# --- Dev server extra origins ----------------------------------------------
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Comma-separated hosts the Next dev server should accept
# cross-origin requests from — needed when you open the dev server from a phone
# or another machine on your LAN. Leave blank otherwise; it has no effect on a
# production build.
FLOWCMS_DEV_ORIGINS=


# === 2. Operational (env bootstrap, admin-panel override) ==================

# --- Public base URL -------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute origin of the public site — used for canonical URLs, sitemaps,
# robots.txt, RSS, and the API client's base URL.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` means this is INLINED AT BUILD TIME into the client bundle, so
# changing it requires a rebuild. `SettingsService.getBaseUrl()` prefers the
# database row when one is set, which is the runtime-configurable path.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Object storage (S3-compatible) ----------------------------------------
# FlowCMS speaks the generic S3 API and sets `forcePathStyle`, so any
# S3-compatible provider works: AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, Garage, Wasabi,
# Backblaze B2, DigitalOcean Spaces, Hetzner.
#
# For self-hosted installs the recommended option is GARAGE
# (https://garagehq.deuxfleurs.fr/) — see docs/architecture/. MinIO is not part
# of the planned FlowCMS installer.
#
# Every field below is overridable per field from Admin > Settings > Storage,
# so a fresh install can be configured entirely in the browser.
S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.com
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=flowcms
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=replace-me
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=replace-me


# === Seeding (local/CLI only — do not set in production) ===================

# Used ONLY by `bun run db:seed` to create the first owner account.
#
# Passing a password as an environment variable leaks it into `docker inspect`,
# shell history, and process listings. Treat these as a local-development
# convenience; a browser-based first-run setup flow is planned to replace them.
OWNER_EMAIL=owner@example.com
OWNER_PASSWORD=replace-me-with-a-strong-password

# ===========================================================================
# Docker
# ===========================================================================
# Everything below matters only when running FlowCMS with Docker Compose.
# Copy this file to .env — Compose reads .env automatically.

# --- Application -----------------------------------------------------------
# Host port. The container always listens on 3000 internally.
FLOWCMS_PORT=3000

# Where the admin panel is served, PUBLICLY. Defaults to /admin.
#
# This is runtime configuration, not a build variant: change it, restart, and
# the panel moves. The image is not rebuilt, and every link, redirect and
# bookmark follows, because nothing in the application hardcodes the path.
#
# The value must start with "/", contain no whitespace and not collide with a
# public route. An invalid one THROWS AT STARTUP rather than falling back —
# a panel silently served somewhere other than where the operator asked for it
# is worse than a container that refuses to start and says why.
#
# It is deliberately NOT a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable. Those are inlined at build
# time, which would make the runtime override a fiction: server routing would
# move while every client-rendered link stayed behind.
FLOWCMS_ADMIN_PATH=/admin

# Public base URL, used for server-side URL generation (canonicals, OG tags,
# sitemaps). Admin > Settings > Global overrides it at runtime.
# Not baked into the browser bundle: the admin panel calls its own origin.
BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Docker Compose selection -----------------------------------------------
# Which Compose files and profiles this installation uses. Compose reads these
# from .env, so `docker compose up -d` needs no -f flags once they are set.
#
# create-flowcms writes them from the topology you chose and deletes the
# overlays you did not. Editing them by hand is how you change topology later:
# add compose.postgres.yml, set DATABASE_DIALECT and DATABASE_URL to match, and
# restart.
#
# The path separator is pinned because Compose's default differs between
# platforms (":" on Linux and macOS, ";" on Windows) and this file is committed.
COMPOSE_PATH_SEPARATOR=:
COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yml

# Comma-separated. "redis" starts the bundled Redis service; leave it empty to
# run without one.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=

# --- Garage (bundled self-hosted S3) ---------------------------------------
# Used only by the bundled `garage` service, which runs by default. An install
# using external S3 switches that service off and can leave these unset — they
# are read by Garage itself and by nothing else.
#
# create-flowcms generates them for you. Generating your own:
#   openssl rand -hex 16       # access key id
#   openssl rand -base64 32    # secret access key
# The placeholders below are NOT usable credentials — replace them.
#
# Compose does NOT enforce their presence, and cannot: it interpolates every
# file before it filters profiles, so a required-variable guard here would also
# fire for installs that switched Garage off. A Garage install that omits them
# fails in Garage's own startup instead.
GARAGE_BUCKET=flowcms
GARAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=replace-me-generate-a-random-access-key-id
GARAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=replace-me-generate-a-random-secret-access-key

# --- External S3 (alternative to Garage) -----------------------------------
# To use a hosted provider instead of Garage, set these and start with:
#   docker compose -f compose.yml -f compose.external-s3.yml up -d
# Left unset, the app targets the bundled Garage service over the Docker
# network (http://garage:3900). All of these can also be set in Admin >
# Settings > Global, which takes precedence over the environment.
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com
# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Future: multi-database support ----------------------------------------
# PLANNED, NOT IMPLEMENTED. FlowCMS currently supports SQLite only, and no code
# reads DATABASE_URL today. PostgreSQL/MySQL/MariaDB arrive with the database
# (this line is obsolete)
# DATABASE_URL=

# --- Docker: remote database credentials ------------------------------------
# Needed ONLY for the matching database topology. The create-flowcms
# installer generates these; for a manual install generate your own, e.g.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
# The values below are placeholders and are not usable credentials.
#
# PostgreSQL — docker compose -f compose.yml -f compose.postgres.yml up -d
# POSTGRES_USER=flowcms
# POSTGRES_DB=flowcms
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=replace-me-generate-a-strong-password
#
# MySQL — docker compose -f compose.yml -f compose.mysql.yml up -d
# MYSQL_USER=flowcms
# MYSQL_DATABASE=flowcms
# MYSQL_PASSWORD=replace-me-generate-a-strong-password
# MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=replace-me-generate-a-different-strong-password
#
# MariaDB — docker compose -f compose.yml -f compose.mariadb.yml up -d
# MARIADB_USER=flowcms
# MARIADB_DATABASE=flowcms
# MARIADB_PASSWORD=replace-me-generate-a-strong-password
# MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD=replace-me-generate-a-different-strong-password
#
# The compose overlays build DATABASE_URL from these, so you do not set
# DATABASE_URL yourself when using a bundled database service.

# --- First owner -------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT read by the running application. These are consumed only by the explicit
# bootstrap command, which must be run once by an operator or the installer:
#
#   docker compose run --rm \
#     -e FLOWCMS_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com \
#     -e FLOWCMS_OWNER_PASSWORD='...' \
#     --entrypoint node app scripts/bootstrap-owner.mjs
#
# There is deliberately no default owner and no default password: `up -d` starts
# a CMS with no accounts at all. Password minimum is 12 characters.
